                        HERALDS OF THE CEMÍ



    On the bank, getting small, a man on a stump wrote in a book.
    He was counting. He was not counting anything that mattered.
    The two stones rang again, and the river took the sound and carried
it out to sea.



What came next arrived in pieces.
   The rest reached me in fragments, some through the stones them‐
selves, some from the people who had been standing in those places
when we later laid our clocks side by side and counted what each of us
had missed. I won’t claim to know their thoughts. Mine were crowded
enough.
   At 10:05, every phone in the hall lit.
   I watched it from the center of the platform: eleven hundred leaves,
one thin bar of rain, and under it, in its old place every eight seconds,
the pressed hand, coming down across the room like a palm across a
table.
   It didn’t land.
   Something was already there.
   The river again. The dark current, the low bank. Niamh had un‐
wrapped the linen. It was a bodhrán, a small round drum, the skin
darkened and scarred by three generations of hands. She braced it
against her knee and struck it once with the heel of her palm, and the
note she made was lower than a drum has any right to go. I felt it in my
molars before I heard it.
   She had said on the phone that she couldn’t promise us a thing. She
stood on the bank of the Boyne a quarter mile from the great mound at
Newgrange, ground she’d walked since childhood, with no permit to
go any nearer and a brother holding a flask whose contents she would
later call an act of aggression. The mound was no machine. It was the
place that had raised her family’s ears. She planted her boots in the wet
grass and played.




